g it back toward the Lean column.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Louisiana remains one of the most Republican-leaning states in presidential politics, having backed the Republican ticket by double digits in recent cycles. With the incumbent president term-limited, both parties will nominate new candidates, but the state's partisan fundamentals point firmly toward the Republican nominee. No candidate-specific polling, fundraising figures, or expert ratings are available yet, and the Republican field is still taking shape, with Senator John Kennedy among those mentioned as possible contenders.\n\nWith no state polls to refine the picture, the rating rests on Louisiana's partisan lean of roughly eleven points toward Republicans. That translates to a clear Republican advantage consistent with a Likely Republican rating, though not yet Safe, since an open seat and an unsettled national environment leave some room for movement. The main uncertainty is how a national swing in either direction interacts with the state's strong Republican baseline.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.108, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.162, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1608, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1621, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1551, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.153, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.155, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.1556, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.1525, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.1509, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.1527, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.1593, "marg